1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,270
Listen to a talk in an anthropology class.

2
00:00:04,270 --> 00:00:12,120
Today, we're examining the intriguing practice of tattooing in different cultures around the world.

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:17,070
Tattoos have been a form of self-expression and identity for thousands of years

4
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:21,170
varying significantly in meaning and style across different societies.

5
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:27,287
In Polynesian cultures, tattoos known as tatau are deeply rooted in tradition

6
00:00:27,287 --> 00:00:32,060
and signify social status, family heritage, and personal achievements.

7
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:35,874
These intricate designs often cover large parts of the body

8
00:00:35,874 --> 00:00:40,250
and are applied using traditional methods that have been handed down through generations.

9
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:46,080
Meanwhile, in Japan, irezumi tattoos are recognized for their bold and vivid designs

10
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:50,820
and while they were once banned by the Japanese government, they are now increasingly

11
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:53,230
popular, especially among young people.

12
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:58,750
Japanese tattoos often depict mythological creatures, flowers,

13
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:04,010
and scenes from folklore, executed with remarkable precision and detail.

14
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:09,260
In contrast, Native American tribes use tattoos for spiritual purposes

15
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:11,050
and as rites of passage.

16
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:16,332
Their designs, mostly geometric and symbolic, were believed to provide protection

17
00:01:16,332 --> 00:01:18,200
and convey tribal affiliations.

18
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:25,850
In contemporary Western society, tattoos have evolved into diverse forms of personal expression, ranging

19
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:28,260
from minimalist designs to elaborate sleeves.

20
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:31,919
They often represent individuality, memories,

21
00:01:31,919 --> 00:01:36,120
or ideologies, reflecting the wearer's unique personality.

22
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:43,404
Anthropologists are fascinated by how these varying practices of tattooing illustrate the broader themes

23
00:01:43,404 --> 00:01:48,060
of cultural identity, societal norms, and individual expression.

24
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:53,959
The ongoing study of tattooing practices offers deep insights into human history

25
00:01:53,959 --> 00:01:57,190
and the ways in which we convey meaning through our bodies.
